# Dédalo nightly backup — reference timer unit (audit S2-35; rebuilt for P0-13 on
# 2026-08-30).
#
# With dedalo-backup-site-builder.service (which it pulls in) it copies the FIVE
# stores engineering/PRODUCTION.md §6 names — the matrix Postgres DB, the RAG
# pgvector DB (if enabled), the media originals, ../private/ (.env secrets, session
# store, ts_state.json) HERE, as DEDALO_USER, and every site-builder INSTANCE on this
# host THERE, as root. One ExecStart per store, in the same order and with the same
# tokens as that table; test/unit/operator_commands_tripwire.test.ts holds the two
# equal, so a store documented there and copied by nothing is impossible.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG WITH THIS FILE BEFORE (P0-13 — every claim measured):
#
#   1. IT READ KEYS THE INSTALLER NEVER WRITES. $DB_PASSWORD/$DB_HOST/$DB_USER/
#      $DB_NAME/$MEDIA_PATH are the TS-native spellings; the wizard then wrote the
#      PHP-catalog ones (DEDALO_PASSWORD_CONN, DEDALO_HOSTNAME_CONN,
#      DEDALO_USERNAME_CONN, DEDALO_DATABASE_CONN), and MEDIA_PATH only when the CLI
#      installer was given --media-path. (Since 2026-10-09 the installer writes the
#      TS-native names; an older or PHP-era .env still holds only the alias.)
#      On a wizard-installed host those expanded to EMPTY STRINGS: a dump attempted
#      as the wrong role against the wrong host, into a file named after nothing.
#      The connection is now passed as the NAMES OF THE KEYS to read, and
#      deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh resolves each one the way the engine's readEnv
#      does — TS-native name first, PHP alias second. It never guesses a value it
#      cannot resolve; it refuses, loudly, naming the key.
#
#   2. `Type=oneshot` WITH FOUR PLAIN ExecStart LINES STOPS AT THE FIRST FAILURE.
#      A database that was down at 03:30 meant the media originals, ../private/ and
#      every museum's site-builder instance were not copied either. Each store now
#      runs through deploy/dedalo-backup-step.sh, whose header explains the shape:
#      every store but the last is `-`-prefixed so its exit status cannot cancel the others,
#      the LAST store carries `--last` and aggregates every recorded status, and the
#      unit FAILS if any store failed. Nothing is papered over: the difference
#      between "a store failed" and "the unit succeeded" is exactly what this
#      arrangement removes.
#
#   3. THERE WAS NO OnFailure= AND NO NOTIFICATION ANYWHERE IN deploy/. A backup
#      that fails silently every night is worse than no backup, because it is
#      believed — the maintenance panel reports a fresh artifact and the operator
#      authorises a code update on the strength of it. See
#      dedalo-backup-alert@.service and deploy/dedalo-backup-alert.sh.
#
#   4. THE MEDIA JOB WAS `rsync -a --delete` INTO ONE GENERATION. A mass deletion
#      was propagated into the only copy of the store PRODUCTION.md §6 calls "the
#      source of truth every derivative rebuilds from" within 24 hours.
#      deploy/dedalo-tree-backup.sh keeps dated, hard-linked generations and states
#      its retention rule (14 by default) in the file.
#
#   5. THE DUMP WAS NEVER READ BACK. deploy/dedalo-db-backup.sh dumps to a `.part`
#      name, reads the archive back end to end with pg_restore, and only then puts
#      it under a `*.backup` name — the suffix newestBackupMtimeMs() scans. A
#      truncated or still-running dump can therefore no longer be the freshest
#      "backup" on disk, which is what made the update panel say "ready".
#
# STORE 5 IS ITS OWN UNIT (2026-10-12), dedalo-backup-site-builder.service: it must
# run as root, and a root (`+`) line HERE would run with this unit's
# EnvironmentFile — a file DEDALO_USER writes (LD_PRELOAD, PATH) — and from scripts
# in a tree DEDALO_USER owns. That unit's header gives the whole reason; nothing in
# this one runs as root.
#
# Enabled by dedalo-backup.timer. VERIFY RESTORES PERIODICALLY: reading a dump back
# proves the bytes are a complete archive, not that it restores into a working
# catalogue. Nothing but an actual restore proves that (PRODUCTION.md §6: quarterly).
#
# Placeholders (rendered by docs/install/manual_host.md, defined in
# deploy/dedalo-ts.service's header): DEDALO_USER, INSTANCE_DIR (the service
# user's — the repo, ../private and backups/ live in it), ROOT_BIN_DIR (root's —
# every script here runs from it, never from the tree a code update moves).

[Unit]
Description=Dedalo nightly backup set (matrix DB + RAG DB + media + private; site-builder instances via its own unit)
# %n is this unit's own name, so the alarm can quote the right journal.
OnFailure=dedalo-backup-alert@%n.service
# Store 5, as root, in its own unit (ordered after this one): the timer runs the set.
Wants=dedalo-backup-site-builder.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=DEDALO_USER
EnvironmentFile=INSTANCE_DIR/private/.env
# Where the per-run store statuses live. systemd creates it before the first step
# and REMOVES it when the unit stops, so one run can never read another's leftovers.
RuntimeDirectory=dedalo-backup
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0750
# Where the PERSISTENT verdict lives (BACKUP_FAILED / LAST_OK). It must match
# dedalo-backup-alert@.service's own Environment= line: both write BACKUP_FAILED and
# only a fully successful run removes it.
Environment=DEDALO_BACKUP_STATE_DIR=INSTANCE_DIR/backups
# WHICH STORES THIS UNIT PROMISES. The aggregate can otherwise only see the
# stores that recorded a status, so a step whose ExecStart never ran — a typo
# here, a missing script, a store commented out by mistake — is invisible and
# the run reports "every store succeeded" over the ones that did run. This list
# must name every ACTIVE ExecStart store below, and nothing else;
# test/unit/operator_commands_tripwire.test.ts holds the two equal.
# UNCOMMENTING store 2 means adding rag_db here in the same edit.
# QUOTED as a whole (campaign 2 L8): systemd splits an Environment= value on
# whitespace, so the unquoted spelling promised `matrix_db` alone and dropped
# the rest as invalid assignments — a truncated list that reported success.
Environment="DEDALO_BACKUP_EXPECTED_STORES=matrix_db media private"
# A dump and a media mirror are a complete, unprotected copy of the collection.
UMask=0077

# 1. Matrix DB — custom format with blobs, read back with pg_restore before it is
#    allowed to be named `*.backup`. --dir-key follows DEDALO_BACKUP_DIR when the
#    operator moved the dumps, because that is the directory the panel lists and the
#    updater's freshness gate scans. --socket-key DB_SOCKET because the engine's
#    pool takes the same route (DB_SOCKET wins over DB_HOST, one rule for every
#    connection: src/config/pg_transport.ts) — a backup that connected by another
#    route would be a second, disagreeing answer to "which server holds the
#    catalogue". Before 2026-10-09 the installer wrote DEDALO_SOCKET_CONN and
#    nothing read it; it is now DB_SOCKET's fallback spelling.
ExecStart=-ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-backup-step.sh run matrix_db ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-db-backup.sh --label timer --dir-key DEDALO_BACKUP_DIR --dir INSTANCE_DIR/private/backups/db --db-key DB_NAME --host-key DB_HOST --port-key DB_PORT --user-key DB_USER --password-key DB_PASSWORD --socket-key DB_SOCKET --pg-dump pg_dump --pg-restore pg_restore
# 2. RAG pgvector DB — UNCOMMENT when the semantic index is enabled. Its keys fall
#    back to the main database's in the ENGINE, but not here on purpose: a backup
#    that silently dumps the catalogue a second time because DEDALO_RAG_DB_NAME is
#    unset would look like a RAG backup and be none.
#ExecStart=-ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-backup-step.sh run rag_db ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-db-backup.sh --label rag --dir INSTANCE_DIR/private/backups/rag --db-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_NAME --host-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_HOSTNAME_CONN --port-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_PORT_CONN --user-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_USERNAME_CONN --password-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_PASSWORD_CONN --socket-key DEDALO_RAG_DB_SOCKET_CONN --pg-dump pg_dump --pg-restore pg_restore
# 3. Media originals — dated generations, hard-linked to the previous one, 14 kept.
#    The source is read from MEDIA_PATH (or its PHP alias); an unset key is a loud
#    refusal, never a guess, because the engine's own default is one of two paths
#    and the wrong guess backs up an empty directory while reporting success.
ExecStart=-ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-backup-step.sh run media ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-tree-backup.sh --label media --source-key MEDIA_PATH --dest INSTANCE_DIR/backups/media --keep 14
# 4. The private dir itself (secrets, sessions, state) — small, always, and
#    generational too: the .env is the file every other restore step reads first, so
#    yesterday's copy is worth exactly as much as the media's. Its own backups/
#    subtree is excluded (store 1 owns those artifacts), and so is export_artifacts/
#    (the default DEDALO_EXPORT_ARTIFACTS_DIR): temporary, owner-only copies of
#    records that the TTL sweep deletes — a 14-generation copy would keep them two
#    weeks past their TTL and outside the owner/read-access re-check
#    (engineering/PRODUCTION.md §15).
#
#    HONEST LIMIT: the session store is a live SQLite database, so its copy can be
#    torn — a restore may lose logged-in sessions, which cost a re-login and nothing
#    else. The files that matter here (.env, ts_state.json) are written atomically by
#    the engine (tmp + rename), so they copy whole or not at all.
#
#    IT IS THE LAST STEP OF THIS UNIT, hence '--last' and no '-': after copying its
#    own store it aggregates every store's recorded status and exits non-zero if ANY
#    of them failed — the unit's verdict is the truth about all of them while no
#    single store can cancel another. If you comment it out, move '--last' to the
#    last ExecStart that remains, or nothing reports the run's verdict.
ExecStart=ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-backup-step.sh run --last private ROOT_BIN_DIR/dedalo-tree-backup.sh --label private --source INSTANCE_DIR/private/ --dest INSTANCE_DIR/backups/private --keep 14 --exclude backups --exclude /export_artifacts
